{{- /*
variant_bundle.gohtml edits what a variant is made of (ADR 0236).

The parts are a textarea holding one variant per line, by its SKU or its id,
then how many one bundle holds; a line with no number holds one. It reads as
the add-ons form does, for the add-ons form's reason: a SKU is what an operator
knows. The module replaces the parts whole and refuses them whole, and the form
comes back with what was typed and the module's sentence. The form carries the
product's version, because the parts are a revision of the product: a save made
after somebody else saved it comes back rather than writing over them.
*/ -}}
{{ define "body" }}
<p><a href="{{ .CancelPath }}">← Back to the variant</a></p>

<h1>Parts of {{ .Variant.Title }}</h1>
<p>{{ .Product.Title }}{{ if .Variant.SKU }} — {{ .Variant.SKU }}{{ end }}</p>
{{ if .Error }}<p role="alert">{{ .Error }}</p>{{ end }}

<form method="post" action="{{ .ActionPath }}">
  <input type="hidden" name="version" value="{{ .Product.Version }}">
  <label>Parts (one variant per line, then how many)
    <textarea name="parts" rows="8">{{ .Text }}</textarea>
  </label>
  <p>Each line is a variant of another product by its SKU or its id, then how
  many of it one bundle holds, from 1 to {{ .QuantityLimit }}; at most
  {{ .Limit }} lines. The bundle has to be counted, not sold past zero and linked
  to no inventory item: its stock is its parts'. An empty list makes it a
  variant of its own again.</p>
  <button type="submit">Save</button>
  <a href="{{ .CancelPath }}">Cancel</a>
</form>
{{ end }}
